SnappyMail release-signing public key

    uid          SnappyMail <releases@snappymail.eu>
    fingerprint  1016 E470 7914 5542 F8BA  1335 4820 8BA1 3290 F3EB
    algorithm    ed25519 (EdDSA), key created 2022-02-16

Where this key comes from:

  * The SnappyMail project ships it in its own repository as
    `build/SnappyMail.asc`, at the release tag the build downloads. The
    key stored here and the one published there carry the same key
    material, the same user id and the same self-signature; they differ
    in three bytes, the packet header tags of the old and the new
    OpenPGP packet format.

  * The same key was fetched from keyserver.ubuntu.com, which is
    independent of the server that serves the release tarballs. It was
    uploaded there in February 2022, more than two years before the
    signature it verifies was made (2024-10-09).

  * Verified: this key produces `Good signature` on
    snappymail-2.38.2.tar.gz against its detached .asc signature.

The project publishes a second, different key in SECURITY.md,
fingerprint 445D265124E6072671E64D0733F868A7E35E8277, for encrypted
vulnerability reports to security@snappymail.eu. That one is not a
release-signing key and is not used here.

Any downgrade of this file back to a placeholder must be treated as a
security incident: it disables SnappyMail release-signature verification
across every future build.

